From a314ba072f6bae30950efdadf4f207a109a16b12 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stefan Hertenberger <erde74@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 15:29:56 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] pipe(3): writes are atomic up to 65536 bytes, not "typically
 32768"

The kernel's queues take a write whole up to Maxatomic, 64*1024
(port/qio.c), and qwrite splits a longer one into pieces of that size;
devpipe writes through qwrite. Maxatomic has been 64 KB at least since
the sources of 2011 (the first import); the page still said 32768.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sys/man/3/pipe | 7 ++++---
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sys/man/3/pipe b/sys/man/3/pipe
index bdbaeba8c..d8d56ef6c 100644
--- a/sys/man/3/pipe
+++ b/sys/man/3/pipe
@@ -24,9 +24,10 @@ Write boundaries are preserved: each read terminates
 when the read buffer is full or after reading the last byte
 of a write, whichever comes first.
 .PP
-Writes are atomic up to a certain size, typically 32768 bytes,
-that is, each write will be delivered in a single read by the
-recipient, provided the receiving buffer is large enough.
+Writes are atomic up to 65536 bytes (the kernel's Maxatomic),
+that is, each write of up to that size will be delivered in a single
+read by the recipient, provided the receiving buffer is large enough;
+a longer write arrives in pieces of that size.
 .PP
 If there are multiple writers, each
 .I write
